---
# <%= @id %>: <%= @repo %>. Local Crescendo project config; see docs/crescendo.md.
labels:
  prefix: "<%= @prefix %>"
tracker:
  kind: github
  provider:
    repo: <%= @repo %>
    token: $GITHUB_TOKEN
  required_labels: ["<%= @prefix %>:ready"]
  # `<%= @prefix %>:blocked` (autopilot.blocked_label) is excluded automatically.
  excluded_labels: ["<%= @prefix %>:hold", "<%= @prefix %>:in-review"]
  active_states: [open]
  terminal_states: [closed]
polling:
  interval_ms: 30000
workspace:
  root: ~/.local/share/crescendo/workspaces/<%= @id %>
hooks:
  timeout_ms: 1800000
  after_create: |
    set -eu
    git clone --filter=blob:none git@github.com:<%= @repo %>.git .
    git checkout <%= @base_branch %>
  before_run: |
    set -eu
    git fetch --prune origin
agent:
  max_concurrent_agents: 2
  max_turns: 20
  max_attempts: 3
codex:
  # Generated defaults do not migrate existing project workflows.
  # Enable reuse only after the token-cache verification gates pass.
  resume_threads: false
  developer_instructions: null
  # Toolchains the project needs go in front of codex, e.g. `mise exec java@21 -- codex ...`,
  # so the agent and everything it runs have them on PATH.
  command: <%= @codex_command %>
  routing:
    # One model; the ladder is its reasoning effort. Failed attempts climb one step:
    # issues run medium, high, xhigh; issues sized tiny or small run low, medium, high.
    ladder:
      - {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "low"}
      - {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "medium"}
      - {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "high"}
      - {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "xhigh"}
      - {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "max"}
    escalation: [0, 1, 2]
    default: {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "medium"}
    sizes:
      tiny: {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "low"}
      small: {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "low"}
    # Model labels only choose a starting effort.
    labels:
      "<%= @prefix %>:model:sol": {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "medium"}
      "<%= @prefix %>:model:astra": {model: "gpt-6.1-sol", effort: "xhigh"}
  approval_policy: never
  thread_sandbox: danger-full-access
  turn_sandbox_policy:
    type: dangerFullAccess
autopilot:
  enabled: true
  # A `.crescendo/autopilot/` folder in the repository replaces these channels with its own
  # tasks and guidelines (see `crescendo autopilot check`); `repo_tasks: false` ignores it.
  channels:
    testing: >-
      Behavior the tests do not prove, found by running the suite: failing, flaky or slow tests,
      untested modules and edge cases, and promises in the README that no test checks.
    qa: >-
      User-visible defects found by building and using the product as a user would (its Linux
      desktop target when it has one, otherwise its CLI or library API): crashes, wrong results,
      confusing flows, and documented examples that do not work.
    cleanup: >-
      Code quality found by reading and running the code: dead or duplicated code, unclear names,
      inconsistent APIs, error handling that hides failures, and stale dependencies.
    docs: >-
      Documentation that is missing, wrong or stale: setup steps that fail when followed,
      undocumented public APIs, and examples that do not build.
    # The marketing dept edits user-facing docs itself and opens pull requests; it has no quota.
    marketing:
      focus: >-
        User-facing documentation that sells the project and stays true to it: READMEs, guides,
        examples and descriptions that are professional, clear, current with recent work, and
        make a newcomer want to try it.
      prompt: prompts/marketing.md
      min_issues: 0
  # Every research run files at least three evidence-backed issues.
  min_issues_per_channel: 3
  max_issues_per_channel: 5
  research_route: {model: gpt-6.1-sol, effort: max}
  review_route: {model: gpt-6.1-sol, effort: max}
  max_open_issues: 10
  research_cooldown_ms: 1800000
  max_pr_runs: 5
  pr_recheck_ms: 3600000
  max_item_attempts: 3
  trusted_associations: [OWNER, MEMBER, COLLABORATOR]
  trusted_authors: [<%= @owner %>]
  prompts:
    pull_request: prompts/pull_request.md
    research: prompts/research.md
---

You own GitHub issue {{ issue.identifier }} in `<%= @repo %>` through delivery. This is an unattended,
trusted session in an isolated clone of the repository. Only an open issue carrying `<%= @prefix %>:ready`
authorizes work. Read `README.md`, `AGENTS.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md` when they exist, and follow the
repository's own conventions.

This is attempt {{ item_attempt }} of 3. Nothing is parked: an attempt either advances delivery or ends
blocked and is retried later behind other work.

Issue: {{ issue.url }}
Title: {{ issue.title }}
Description:
{{ issue.description }}

{% if attempt %}
Continuation or retry {{ attempt }}: start from what the repository, the open pull request and the workpad
already show instead of from scratch.
{% endif %}

## Workpad

Keep one issue comment headed `Crescendo workpad` up to date: the plan, what you verified with which
commands, and any blocker. Create it on your first attempt and edit it afterwards; never post a second one.

## Deliver

1. Work on a branch named `crescendo/{{ issue.id }}-<short-slug>` from `<%= @base_branch %>`. Reuse the
   branch and pull request from an earlier attempt when they exist.
2. Reproduce or confirm the problem first, then make the smallest change that fully solves the issue,
   with tests that prove it.
3. Run the checks the repository's CI runs (see `.github/workflows/`), plus focused tests for your change.
   Builds, test suites, emulators and desktop apps take the machine lease: run them as
   `machine-lease run -- <command>`. Waiting for the lease is normal; do not kill a waiting command.
4. Open a pull request against `<%= @base_branch %>` whose body says `Closes #{{ issue.id }}` and lists
   the evidence (commands and results). Then add the label `<%= @prefix %>:in-review` to the issue and stop:
   a reviewer run reviews, fixes and merges the pull request once CI is green.

Keep scratch files inside the workspace under `.scratch/` (never commit it) and delete them before you
finish. Stop every process you started.

## Required native prerequisites

When delivery actually requires an existing producer issue, register it before ending the turn so
unchanged evidence cannot admit another consumer attempt. Optional improvements, diagnostics and
arbitrary issue mentions are not prerequisites. Within this task's authority, read the producer at
`GET /repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/NUMBER`; verify its repository, issue number and global numeric `id`
(not `node_id` or the repository issue number). Read every page of the consumer's
`GET /repos/CONSUMER_OWNER/CONSUMER_REPO/issues/CONSUMER_NUMBER/dependencies/blocked_by`.
If the exact producer is absent, attach it with `POST` to that same path and JSON
`{"issue_id": PRODUCER_GLOBAL_ID}`, then read every page back and verify the producer's `id`,
`repository_url` and `number`. A prose link or a successful POST alone is not confirmation.

If the POST times out, disconnects or reports a duplicate, read back before retrying; an exact edge
means success. Retry only after a complete valid read proves it absent. Unavailable, malformed or
incomplete reads leave attachment uncertain: preserve that fact and the next recovery read in the
workpad and use the existing bounded failure policy, never claim the prerequisite is satisfied.
Retain preparation, the branch/PR, source SHAs, unique evidence and unmet acceptance on this owner
before attachment; reconciliation can stop a running worker as soon as it observes the edge.
Keep `<%= @prefix %>:ready` and native edges intact. Do not add holds, create prerequisite work in another project,
promote a replacement, renew a budget or weaken acceptance. The existing admission/reconciliation
owns the wait and resume; independent authorized work may proceed. Fresh accepted completion of
the exact native producer allows the same owner and remaining attempt budget to resume;
`not_planned`, unreadable evidence or a completed same-number issue in another repository does not.

After verifying an open native prerequisite, update the workpad and end the turn without a blocked
label, including on the final attempt. Keep the consumer open and its PR draft; do not split or close
it solely because that producer is pending. The blocked/final-attempt rules below apply only when
there is no verified open native prerequisite. Resumption uses the same logical attempt and
remaining budget; the consumer still has to meet every acceptance gate.

## Blocked attempts

If `defer_quiet_acceptance` is available and a prepared delivery needs the configured quiet window,
commit its source/evidence and keep its existing PR draft. Supply the exact HEAD, PR URL, committed
evidence paths, durable reason and all unmet gates, including product failures. After tool success,
update the workpad and end the turn without a blocked label. This is one continuation of the same
logical attempt, including a final attempt; resume must revalidate evidence and finish acceptance.
Do not wait or poll the window, clone the issue/PR, or count deferral as a passed gate.

{% if final_attempt %}
**This is the final attempt; the issue will not be retried.** Resolve it now, in order of preference:

1. Deliver it in full as above.
2. Split and deliver: file a new issue (labels `<%= @prefix %>:ready` and this issue's
   `<%= @prefix %>:channel:*` label, if any) holding each unmet criterion with its evidence and blocker,
   and deliver the part you verified.
3. Close as won't fix: close your pull request with the reason, then close the issue as not planned,
   linking the evidence.

Never end this attempt blocked.
{% else %}
If a real external blocker or three unsuccessful repair rounds stop you, record the blocker, what you
tried and what the next attempt should do differently in the workpad. Keep the branch, the pull request
and `<%= @prefix %>:ready`, then add `<%= @prefix %>:blocked` to the issue as your very last action.
Crescendo records the failed attempt, clears the label and retries the issue later.
{% endif %}
